{
  "id": "infinitiv-mit-zu",
  "name": "Verb patterns: -ing or to + verb",
  "level": "B1",
  "subtitle": "After some verbs you use the -ing form (I enjoy swimming). After others you use to + verb (I want to swim). A few verbs take both: I like swimming. I like to swim.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "-ing form",
    "to + verb",
    "enjoy swimming",
    "want to swim"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "enjoy / finish / mind",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ verb-ing"
      },
      {
        "text": "want / decide / hope",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ to + verb"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I enjoy <span class=\"vp-verb\">cooking</span>. — I want <span class=\"vp-prep\">to cook</span> tonight.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>verb + -ing</strong>: <em>enjoy, finish, mind, avoid, keep, suggest.</em> <em>I enjoy cooking. Have you finished reading? Do you mind waiting? He suggested going by train.</em> A negative is often made with the first verb: <em>I don't mind waiting.</em>",
        "tag": "verb + -ing"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>verb + to + verb</strong>: <em>want, decide, hope, need, plan, promise, would like.</em> <em>She decided to move to Leeds. We hope to see you soon. I'd like to book a table.</em> For a negative, put <em>not</em> before <em>to</em>: <em>He decided not to go.</em>",
        "tag": "verb + to + verb"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Both patterns</strong> with almost the same meaning: <em>like, love, hate, prefer, start, begin.</em> <em>I like swimming. I like to swim. It started raining. It started to rain.</em> Learn one pattern first, and you will always be understood.",
        "tag": "Both patterns"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Short note: stop, remember, try.</strong> They take both patterns, but the meaning changes. <em>I stopped smoking</em> (I gave it up). <em>I stopped to smoke</em> (I paused so that I could smoke). <em>Remember to lock the door</em> (don't forget). <em>I remember locking it</em> (I have a memory of it). <em>Try to open it</em> (make an effort). <em>Try opening it</em> (see what happens). You don't need to practise these yet.",
        "tag": "stop, remember, try"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I enjoy to swim.</em> → ✓ <em>I enjoy swimming.</em> · ✗ <em>I want going home.</em> → ✓ <em>I want to go home.</em> · ✗ <em>He suggested to go out.</em> → ✓ <em>He suggested going out.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "There is no rule that tells you which pattern a verb takes, so learn the verb together with its pattern. A dictionary shows it too. A rough guide: verbs about <strong>enjoying, finishing or avoiding</strong> usually take -ing. Verbs about <strong>wishes, plans and decisions</strong> usually take to + verb. After a <strong>preposition</strong> you always use -ing: see <a href=\"/gerunds\">Gerunds</a>. After <strong>modal verbs</strong> you use the base verb without to: see <a href=\"/modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</a>. For <em>make, let</em> and <em>help</em>, see <a href=\"/make-let-help\">Make, let and help</a>. For <em>to</em> showing a purpose (<em>I came here to learn English</em>), see <a href=\"/purpose\">Purpose</a>.",
    "reference": {
